L358: # The ARRIVE guidelines 2.0: author checklist
L359: ## The ARRIVE Essential 10
L360: These items are the basic minimum to include in a manuscript. Without this information, readers and reviewers
L361: cannot assess the reliability of the findings.
L362: **Item** **Recommendation**
L363: **Section/line**
L364: **number, or reason**
L365: **for not reporting**
L366: |Study design 1 For each experiment, provide brief details of study design including:<br>a. The groups being compared, including control groups. If no control group has<br>been used, the rationale should be stated.<br>b. The experimental unit (e.g. a single animal, litter, or cage of animals).|Experimental<br>design lines 93-<br>97 /Table 1<br>Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92|
L367: |---|---|
L368: |**Sample size**<br>2<br>a. Specify the exact number of experimental units allocated to each group, and the<br>total number in each experiment. Also indicate the total number of animals used.<br>b. Explain how the sample size was decided. Provide details of any_a priori_ sample<br>size calculation, if done.|Experimental design<br>lines91-95 /Table 1<br>Experimental design<br>lines93-99 /Table 1|
L369: |**Inclusion and**<br>**exclusion**<br>**criteria**<br>3<br>a. Describe any criteria used for including and excluding animals (or experimental<br>units) during the experiment, and data points during the analysis. Specify if these<br>criteria were established_a priori._ If no criteria were set, state this explicitly.<br>b. For each experimental group, report any animals, experimental units or data points<br>not included in the analysis and explain why. If there were no exclusions, state so.<br>c. For each analysis, report the exact value of_n_ in each experimental group.|no criteria were<br>utilized<br>no used exclusions<br>Experimental<br>design lines93-97|
L370: |**Randomisation**<br>4<br>a. State whether randomisation was used to allocate experimental units to control<br>and treatment groups. If done, provide the method used to generate the<br>randomisation sequence.<br>b. Describe the strategy used to minimise potential confounders such as the order<br>of treatments and measurements, or animal/cage location. If confounders were<br>not controlled, state this explicitly.|randomisation<br>sequence was no<br>maintained<br>Experimental<br>design<br>lines93-97 /Table 1|
L371: |**Blinding**<br>5<br>Describe who was aware of the group allocation at the diferent stages of the<br>experiment (during the allocation, the conduct of the experiment, the outcome<br>assessment, and the data analysis).|First author|
L372: |**Outcome**<br>**measures**<br>6<br>a. Clearly defne all outcome measures assessed (e.g. cell death, molecular markers,<br>or behavioural changes).<br>b. For hypothesis-testing studies, specify the primary outcome measure, i.e. the<br>outcome measure that was used to determine the sample size.|Materials and<br>methods/line 69-177<br>not applyable|
L373: |**Statistical**<br>**methods**<br>7<br>a. Provide details of the statistical methods used for each analysis, including<br>software used.<br>b. Describe any methods used to assess whether the data met the assumptions of<br>the statistical approach, and what was done if the assumptions were not met.|Statistical<br>analysis/line173-177<br>Statistical<br>analysis/line173-177|
L374: |**Experimental**<br>**animals**<br>8<br>a. Provide species-appropriate details of the animals used, including species, strain<br>and substrain, sex, age or developmental stage, and, if relevant, weight.<br>b. Provide further relevant information on the provenance of animals, health/immune<br>status, genetic modifcation status, genotype, and any previous procedures.|Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92<br>Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92|
L375: |**Experimental**<br>**procedures**<br>9<br>For each experimental group, including controls, describe the procedures in enough<br>detail to allow others to replicate them, including:<br>a. What was done, how it was done and what was used.<br>b. When and how often.<br>c. Where (including detail of any acclimatisation periods).<br>d. Why (provide rationale for procedures).|Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92<br>Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92<br>Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92<br>Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92|
L376: |**Results**<br>10<br>For each experiment conducted, including independent replications, report:<br>a. Summary/descriptive statistics for each experimental group, with a measure of<br>variability where applicable (e.g. mean and SD, or median and range).<br>b. If applicable, the efect size with a confdence interval.|not applicable<br>Result/line178-23<br>7|
L377: ## The Recommended Set
L378: These items complement the Essential 10 and add important context to the study. Reporting the items in both sets
L379: represents best practice.
L380: **Item** **Recommendation**
L381: **Section/line**
L382: **number, or reason**
L383: **for not reporting**
L384: |Abstract 11 Provide an accurate summary of the research objectives, animal species, strain<br>and sex, key methods, principal findings, and study conclusions.|Acstract/line 18-35|
L385: |---|---|
L386: |**Background**<br>12<br>a. Include sufcient scientifc background to understand the rationale and<br>context for the study, and explain the experimental approach.<br>b. Explain how the animal species and model used address the scientifc<br>objectives and, where appropriate, the relevance to human biology.|introduction/ line<br>38-68<br> introduction/line38-68|
L387: |**Objectives**<br>13<br>Clearly describe the research question, research objectives and, where<br>appropriate, specifc hypotheses being tested.|introduction/line65-68|
L388: |**Ethical**<br>**statement**<br>14<br>Provide the name of the ethical review committee or equivalent that has approved<br>the use of animals in this study, and any relevant licence or protocol numbers (if<br>applicable). If ethical approval was not sought or granted, provide a justifcation.|Ethics<br>statement/<br>line 70-72|
L389: |**Housing and**<br>**husbandry**<br>15<br>Provide details of housing and husbandry conditions, including any environmental<br>enrichment.|Experimental animals and parasites|
L390: |**Animal care and**<br>**monitoring**<br>16<br>a. Describe any interventions or steps taken in the experimental protocols to<br>reduce pain, sufering and distress.<br>b. Report any expected or unexpected adverse events.<br>c. Describe the humane endpoints established for the study, the signs that were<br>monitored and the frequency of monitoring. If the study did not have humane<br>endpoints, state this.|Experimental animals and<br>parasites/line 83-92<br>not recorded<br>not applicaple|
L391: |**Interpretation/**<br>**scientifc**<br>**implications**<br>17<br>a. Interpret the results, taking into account the study objectives and hypotheses,<br>current theory and other relevant studies in the literature.<br>b. Comment on the study limitations including potential sources of bias,<br>limitations of the animal model, and imprecision associated with the results.|discussion/ line<br>238-390<br>discussion/ line 238-390<br>conclusion line 291-301|
L392: |**Generalisability/**<br>**translation**<br>18<br>Comment on whether, and how, the fndings of this study are likely to generalise<br>to other species or experimental conditions, including any relevance to human<br>biology (where appropriate).|not applicaple|
L393: |**Protocol**<br>**registration**<br>19<br>Provide a statement indicating whether a protocol (including the research<br>question, key design features, and analysis plan) was prepared before the study,<br>and if and where this protocol was registered.|not applicaple|
L394: |**Data access**<br>20<br>Provide a statement describing if and where study data are available.|lines 309-311|
L395: |**Declaration of**<br>**interests**<br>21<br>a. Declare any potential conficts of interest, including fnancial and non-fnancial.<br>If none exist, this should be stated.<br>b. List all funding sources (including grant identifer) and the role of the funder(s)<br>in the design, analysis and reporting of the study.|lines 312-313<br>lines 302-303|
